        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "The opening sentence of Lincoln's second paragraph, which takes a nominalization as its subject and then follows it with a passive verb, is conspicuously impersonal: all thoughts were... directed. Who thought? Who directed? Then for two short sentences, Lincoln switches to the direct style we expect from him, a style in which subjects are concrete human characters:",
